.TH LOGPRINT 1 "2026-05-12" "logprint 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
logprint \- print log files on an Epson TM-T20IV
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B logprint
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fIFILE\fR
.br
.B logprint
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\fR
.br
.B logprint
\fB\-c\fR \fICONFIG.toml\fR [\fIFILE\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B logprint
reads a log file (or standard input) and prints it on an Epson TM-T20IV
(or a compatible ESC/POS receipt printer) over the raw port 9100.
.PP
Lines are wrapped before they are sent to the printer. By default this
means 48 characters per line (font A, 80 mm paper). Continuation lines
are indented by two spaces. Very long words are hard-split; otherwise
wrapping prefers word boundaries.
.PP
In the normal mode only paper is fed forward at the end, nothing is cut.
Cutting happens with
.B \-cut
(after a single run) or with
.B \-cut\-on\-exit
(also when a follow run finishes).
.PP
ANSI color codes, control characters and common Unicode symbols are
stripped or replaced so that log content cannot smuggle in ESC/POS
commands. Output uses CP858 by default (German umlauts and Euro);
other code pages are available.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " CONFIG.toml"
Read a TOML configuration file and apply the values set there. This lets
you start one instance per config file (for example one printer in the
kitchen, another in the warehouse), each with its own invocation:
.br
.B logprint \-c kitchen.toml
.br
.B logprint \-c warehouse.toml
.PP
Explicitly set command-line flags take precedence over the file; keys
missing from the file keep their default values. Allowed keys are (flat
or under a
.B [printer]
section):
.BR host ,
.BR port ,
.BR follow ,
.BR from_start ,
.BR width ,
.BR font ,
.BR indent ,
.BR codepage ,
.BR input_encoding ,
.BR timestamps ,
.BR no_header ,
.BR feed ,
.BR cut ,
.BR cut_on_exit ,
.BR max_lines
and optionally
.BR file .
Default: no config file.
.TP
.BI \-host " HOST"
IP address or hostname of the printer. Default:
.IR 192.168.1.185 .
.TP
.BI \-port " PORT"
TCP port for raw printing. Default:
.IR 9100 .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-follow
Tail the file like
.B tail \-f
and print only new lines. Default: off (single run).
.TP
.B \-from-start
Only together with
.BR \-follow :
print the existing content of the file first, then keep following.
Default: off.
.TP
.BI \-width " N"
Characters per line. Default: 48 for font A, 64 for font B.
For 58 mm paper, 32 (font A) or 42 (font B) are common.
.TP
.BI \-font " a|b"
Font:
.B a
(48 columns) or
.B b
(64 columns, smaller type). Default:
.BR a .
.TP
.BI \-indent " STRING"
Indentation for wrapped continuation lines. Default: two spaces.
Use
.BI \-indent " \(dq\(dq"
to disable the indentation.
.TP
.BI \-codepage " NAME"
Code page of the printer. Supported:
.BR cp437 ,
.BR cp850 ,
.BR cp852 ,
.BR cp858 ,
.BR cp866
and
.BR cp1252 .
Default:
.BR cp858 .
.TP
.BI \-input\-encoding " NAME"
Encoding of the log file. Supported:
.BR utf\-8 ,
.BR utf8 ,
.BR latin1 ,
.B iso\-8859\-1
and the code pages above (for example
.BR cp1252 ).
Default:
.BR utf\-8 .
.TP
.B \-timestamps
Prefix each line with a time stamp of the form
.B [HH:MM:SS].
Default: off.
.TP
.B \-no\-header
Do not print a header with file name, start time and column width.
Default: off (the header is printed).
.TP
.BI \-feed " N"
Number of lines fed forward at the end. Default: 3.
.TP
.B \-cut
Perform a partial cut at the end of a single run. Default: no cut. In
follow mode this option is ignored; use
.B \-cut\-on\-exit
there.
.TP
.B \-cut\-on\-exit
Send a feed and a partial cut when the program exits, also in follow
mode (for example after Ctrl-C or SIGTERM). Not triggered by SIGKILL or
a power loss. The number of feed lines is controlled by
.BR \-feed .
Default: off.
.TP
.BI \-max\-lines " N"
Stop after
.I N
printed lines. Default: 0 (unlimited).
.TP
.B \-feed-only
Do not print anything; only feed paper and optionally cut, then exit. The
number of feed lines comes from
.BR \-feed
(default 3); add
.B \-cut
or
.B \-cut\-on\-exit
for a partial cut. No FILE argument is required. This is useful after a
paper jam, before a cut, or when you only want to advance the paper.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-help
Print the short help with all options.
.PP
Long options may alternatively be written with a double dash (for example
.BR \-\-follow ).
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
The
.B \-c
option reads a TOML file. Every key can be written flat or grouped under
a
.B [printer]
section. A minimal file looks like this:
.PP
.nf
# kitchen.toml
[printer]
host       = "192.168.1.185"
port       = 9100
font       = "a"
width      = 48
codepage   = "cp858"
follow     = false
timestamps = true
feed       = 3
file       = "/var/log/app.log"
.fi
.PP
Precedence is: defaults < config file < explicitly set command-line flags.
A missing key keeps its default; a positional FILE argument overrides a
.B file
key from the config. The example below starts
.B logprint
from the kitchen config but, for this single run, targets a different
printer and prints a different file:
.PP
.nf
logprint \-c kitchen.toml \-host 192.168.1.200 /tmp/demo.log
.fi
.PP
Here the
.B \-host
flag overrides the value from
.B kitchen.toml
and the positional
.I /tmp/demo.log
overrides the
.B file
key, while width, font, codepage and the rest still come from the file.
.SH STANDARD DEFAULTS
When no options are given, the following applies:
.TP
.B Target
192.168.1.185:9100
.TP
.B Mode
single run, print the whole file (no follow)
.TP
.B Start position
beginning of the file
.TP
.B Line width
48 characters (font A, 80 mm)
.TP
.B Font
a
.TP
.B Indentation
two spaces
.TP
.B Code page
cp858 (umlauts and Euro)
.TP
.B Input encoding
utf-8
.TP
.B Header
on (file name, start time, column width)
.TP
.B Timestamps
off
.TP
.B Trailing feed
3 lines
.TP
.B Cut at end
off
.TP
.B Cut on exit
off
.TP
.B Maximum lines
unlimited
.TP
.B Config file
none (option \fB\-c\fR)
.PP
Concretely: without options
.B logprint
prints a file once, completely, with header, 48 characters per line,
no timestamps, three lines of trailing feed and no cut.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B logprint /var/log/syslog
Print the file once, completely.
.TP
.B logprint \-f /var/log/myapp.log
Print only new lines, tailing the file.
.TP
.B logprint \-f \-from\-start /var/log/myapp.log
Print the existing content first, then keep following.
.TP
.B logprint \-f \-cut\-on\-exit /var/log/myapp.log
Tail the file and, when it exits (Ctrl-C/SIGTERM), send a feed and a
partial cut.
.TP
.B tail \-F /var/log/syslog | logprint \-
Print from a pipe; also survives log rotation handled by
.BR tail .
.TP
.B logprint \-font b \-width 64 /var/log/app.log
Print with font B and 64 characters per line.
.TP
.B logprint \-timestamps \-max\-lines 50 /var/log/app.log
Print the first 50 lines with time stamps.
.TP
.B logprint \-feed-only
Advance the paper by the configured feed (default 3 lines) without printing
anything.
.TP
.B logprint \-feed-only \-feed 5 \-cut
Advance the paper 5 lines and then cut, without printing anything.
.SS "Mixing a config file with command-line flags"
.TP
.B logprint \-c kitchen.toml
Start everything from
.B kitchen.toml
(such as
.BR host ,
.BR width ,
.BR font ,
.BR codepage ,
.BR follow ).
Useful when the same configuration is started multiple times (for
example as a systemd unit).
.TP
.B logprint \-c kitchen.toml \-host 192.168.1.200
Start from
.B kitchen.toml
but override the printer address for this run.
.TP
.B logprint \-c kitchen.toml \-width 64 \-font b
Start from
.B kitchen.toml
but print wider (font B, 64 columns) for this run.
.TP
.B logprint \-c kitchen.toml \-f \-no\-header /tmp/demo.log
Start from
.B kitchen.toml
but enable follow mode, suppress the header and print
.I /tmp/demo.log
instead of the file configured in the file.
.TP
.B logprint \-c kitchen.toml \-timestamps
Start from
.B kitchen.toml
and enable per-line timestamps, overriding the file value.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Runtime error, for example the printer is unreachable or a write failed.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid command-line arguments.
.SH REMARKS
.PP
In the printer's web config,
.B Secure Printing
must be disabled. If it is active, external print jobs are accepted but
not printed; the printer then reports
.B EX_TIMEOUT
or "no printer response".
.PP
In follow mode the TCP connection stays open. The printer drops it after
90 seconds of idle time. On the next log entry,
.B logprint
reconnects automatically and initializes the printer again (code page,
font, alignment).
.PP
Log rotation and truncation are detected in follow mode: if the inode
changes or the file becomes smaller than the already-read position,
.B logprint
reopens the file.
.PP
The printer has no authentication for raw printing. Port 9100 should
therefore not be reachable from the internet, only from the LAN or via
VPN.
